    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d608">

                <pb facs="0684" n="666" sameAs="frus:frus1946v11/pg666"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">821.7962/2–1146</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by Mr. Leo G. Cyr of the Aviation Division
                        to the Acting Assistant Chief of the Division of American Republics Analysis
                        and Liaison</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Halle</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extracts]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-02-26">February 26, 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Reference your chit of February 14,<anchor n="43" xml:id="d608-f1"/> requesting
                    whether AV can supply the answers to the questions raised by Mr. Briggs<anchor
                        n="44" xml:id="d608-f2"/> in his attached note. Mr. Briggs’ questions are
                    answered in order, as follows:</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>5. AV is not in a position to state what military rights may be desired in
                    airfields in Colombia.<anchor n="45" xml:id="d608-f3"/></p>
                <p>By virtue of an exchange of notes signed in 1929<anchor n="46" xml:id="d608-f4"/>
                    by the Governments of the United States and Colombia, American commercial
                    aircraft enjoy the right “to fly along the Atlantic and Pacific coasts of
                    Colombia and over the territory immediately adjacent thereto; to land on land or
                    water, fuel, make repairs, and ship and discharge passengers, mail, and cargo in
                    the Atlantic and Pacific ports of Colombia where there are authorities charged
                    with carrying out the pertinent regulations, subject to regulations and
                    provisions equivalent to those established for commercial aircraft of Colombian
                    registry in the enclosures to this note.”</p>
                <p>The United States government has proposed a new bilateral agreement to Colombia,
                    but it appears that the Colombians want to exercise reciprocal rights under the
                    1929 agreement first. Two applications for certificates of public convenience
                    and necessity have been filed with CAB by Colombian carriers, under the 1929
                    agreement.</p>
            </div>
